<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hallstatt-culture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hallstatt culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hallstatt period</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hallstatt culture was the main archaeological culture of western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, from about 800 to 450 BCE, and is commonly associated with speakers of Proto-Celtic. It grew out of the Urnfield culture and is named after Hallstatt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, where Johann Georg Ramsauer found a cemetery in 1846 and the salt mines preserved textiles, wood and leather. Towards its end people of high rank were buried under mounds with Greek imports, as at Vix and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> followed in much of its area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture.jpg" alt="Hallstatt culture" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dbachmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallstatt_LaTene.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
